[{:db/id 17592186083177 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d15324a3-fbaa-5825-b749-4eff694b4d05" "question-61f38221-2cec-5289-9323-193f490aea4a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186409785 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186409786 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d15324a3-fbaa-5825-b749-4eff694b4d05" :question/id #uuid "d15324a3-fbaa-5825-b749-4eff694b4d05" :question/math-academy-id "q-183878" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & ={{field-12}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{n}\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all {{field-15}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{1} & {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$ \n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5({5}^{k}) & 4({5}^{k})+({5}^{k}-1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1} & {5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all $n\\ge 1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-66e3e3c5-68f4-5584-ab5f-659bf98be832" "answer-field-325daa01-196a-51ea-a54e-e3c25750471e" "answer-field-452dc505-9639-52e9-802b-504b91481889" "answer-field-92af74ed-0ffd-52a3-b67b-2fa3d06030c8" "answer-field-4ab304e0-7697-5f6e-81f2-fba124d96a17" "answer-field-cef68933-0d26-56ef-9914-b23570df9634" "answer-field-479360cd-aee1-57f4-b414-a7bcb8403cec" "answer-field-9001bfcb-7b46-546e-994d-8b4b1c3ae8dd" "answer-field-c63baf61-973f-5c74-8a0b-848888371d8d" "answer-field-b70ad14e-68b7-5e09-98e6-1036f95499cd" "answer-field-713623b1-8bfd-5531-8968-fa4021f21b78" "answer-field-ea38e294-3965-5505-9e35-91a41d781483" "answer-field-9a784818-345d-5012-92c2-a583bb77a257" "answer-field-ddde9c74-8ce3-5393-98df-21bcc8a76326" "answer-field-a4bec58a-1a60-53d0-9376-ff6b59e33d35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-66e3e3c5-68f4-5584-ab5f-659bf98be832" :answer-field/id #uuid "66e3e3c5-68f4-5584-ab5f-659bf98be832" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :ans