 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & ={{field-12}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all {{field-15}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all $n\\in N.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8de3cd8c-a13b-5548-b754-cad95aff44af" "answer-field-1c6ead39-e24e-5181-91e8-543cf96c8148" "answer-field-193390c2-e237-5864-89f2-4f094da96612" "answer-field-96d421a9-42a0-5fe1-bfdd-28c06f747b11" "answer-field-564a6493-fca9-5e8c-bb2b-099d7657c103" "answer-field-c63a345a-8095-5ca1-9a2b-bcfc639682de" "answer-field-606ce08b-c410-59e5-b7b3-1256e8245009" "answer-field-bacd9992-ac69-5051-8a37-f0756fd85513" "answer-field-9ddc8976-375d-55ab-a490-01575de1062b" "answer-field-6fd2376a-f94f-5940-b83b-7a2d1766f8e0" "answer-field-1137266b-e42a-519e-99f6-c36b734d57c8" "answer-field-3be9ab45-1931-5452-9012-7acfd859942b" "answer-field-7cf50fea-1b7f-5618-a293-da7d39025a0e" "answer-field-043d0edd-df37-5d91-91f6-b1a4c1eb3ae2" "answer-field-d4e9d017-a142-50f4-82a8-0b219e05832b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8de3cd8c-a13b-5548-b754-cad95aff44af" :answer-field/id #uuid "8de3cd8c-a13b-5548-b754-cad95aff44af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-dbb8e964-5895-55f7-ad2c-0173d8fa0cdd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fae92ea9-c846-5545-83d5-1500324f8618" "answer-dbb8e964-5895-55f7-ad2c-0173d8fa0cdd" "answer-de497721-a003-5004-9dc2-8bff55a7eecc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fae92ea9-c846-5545-83d5-1500324f8618" :answer/id #uuid "fae92ea9-c846-5545-83d5-1500324f8618" :answer/type :answer.type/mat